<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uthman ibn Affan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">caliph 644–<a href="/years/656">656 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Uthman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uthman ibn Affan was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, ruling from 644 to 656 CE. His reign saw continued expansion and the compilation of a standard text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, but accusations of favouring his Umayyad kinsmen provoked growing discontent. His assassination in 656 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, a civil war that accounts of the Rashidun Caliphate treat as central to its decline.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Uthman was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> around 576 into the Banu Abd Shams clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> and its leading family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-umayya">Banu Umayya</a>, then the most powerful family of the Quraysh. According to the account of the 9th-century scholar Ibn Sa'd, he became the fourth convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Ibn Ishaq holds that Abu Bakr brought him to Muhammad.</p><p>Under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> he migrated to Abyssinia with his wife Ruqayya, a daughter of Muhammad, in April 615, and in 622 the couple were among the third group of Muslims to migrate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. His marriages to two of Muhammad's daughters, Ruqayya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umm-kulthum">Umm Kulthum</a>, earned him the title Dhu al-Nurayn, the Possessor of Two Lights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before dying, the caliph Umar appointed a council of six early companions, among them Uthman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, to choose his successor, and Uthman, then aged 68, was elected. His rule saw expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, and westward to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and he delegated military authority to kinsmen such as Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan. In 649 Mu'awiya was allowed to set up a navy, which defeated the Byzantine fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-masts">Battle of the Masts</a> in 655.</p><p>Around 650, noticing differences in how the Qur'an was recited as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread, he ordered a committee headed by Zayd ibn Thabit to prepare a standard text from the copy kept by Hafsa bint Umar. Copies were sent to the main cities and other versions were ordered burnt. Shi'a Muslims use the same Qur'an but do not believe it was first compiled by Uthman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Discontent grew over his fiscal policies and his appointment of relatives to office. Around 654 he called his twelve provincial governors to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, and rebel contingents of about 1,000 each came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. They besieged his house; his supporters, who outnumbered them, asked to fight, but he refused in order to avoid bloodshed among Muslims. On 17 June 656 rebels climbed in from a neighbouring house and killed him as he recited the Qur'an. Sunni tradition counts him the third rightly guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, and Sunni historians view his rule positively, particularly his leniency. Others accuse him of corruption.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uthman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Uthman ibn Affan</b>, caliph from 644 to 656 CE, the Quranic text was standardised into a single authoritative recension.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebels besieging the house of <b>Uthman ibn Affan</b>, the third Rashidun caliph, who had refused their demand for a new caliph, set it on fire, broke in and killed him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-17">17 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=651" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Uthman ibn Affan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=651">What was here in 651 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
